|~ `::tagged`, the kind of every value under a tag, which the walk of a
   tagged value reaches after its own tags, and what is declared under
   it: the verbs of tagged values.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:tagged
  |~~ A value under a tag is a payload read by the kind its tag names,
      `::Box[1 2]`, `::Box{:a 1}`, `::Box#5`, `::Box"s"`: the tag says
      how to read the data beneath, and the data keeps its shape, so the
      verbs of the payload's kind still serve it, `::Box[1 2] | count`
      answering `2`. Tags stack and read from the outside in,
      `::Box::Crate{:a 1} | tag` answering `::Box`.

      It is written as the tag touching its payload, whatever brackets
      hold the payload, or made by `tag`, `[1 2] | tag ::Box` answering
      `::Box[1 2]`. A tag declared as a kind runs over the payload its
      constructor, where its slot holds one, and its invariants, and one
      nothing declares lays itself as it is. `payload` takes the outer tag off, and
      `within ~(…)` edits the payload under the tag, which checks it
      again. JSON writes it as an object of its `$tag` and its
      `payload`.

      A verb finds a value under a tag through its tags from the outside
      in, then this kind, then the kind of its payload, so every value
      under a tag reaches the verbs of this kind, a reader's kind adds
      verbs over the core's, and a vector under a tag stays a vector.
      ::snippet~(::tagged | manifest) lists its verbs.
      ::link~(::tag | doc) ::link~(::construct | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ No value: `::tagged` names verbs. ~~| #[]
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::tagged` names the verbs of every value under a tag and takes
            no payload: a value goes under a tag by naming the tag,
            `5 | tag ::Box`.
        ~~|
    | proves :reachesPayload
        |~~ A box answers `payload`. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])
    | proves :readsAsAConstructStep
        |~~ The step `::Box[1 2]` is a `::construct`. ~~|
        ~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | tag | eq ::construct)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAValue
        |~~ No value is put under `::tagged`, and a value under a tag is of
            it already.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(5 | tag ::tagged !| tag) ~(::Box#5 | tag ::tagged)] | eq [::tagged/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::Box#5])

:tagged/within
  |~~ Answers the subject with code run beneath its tag: the quote the
      call gives runs against the payload, as `apply` runs it, and its answer
      goes back under the tag, `::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse)` answering
      `::Box[2 1]`. The tag is laid once through its kind, on the answer
      of the code, so the steps between may break what the tag holds of its
      payload: a set's constructor puts the vector the code answered in
      order, `#[3 1] | within ~(get [/0 /1 /0])` answering `#[1 3]`, and a
      tag whose invariant the answer breaks refuses it. The names the
      code binds stay inside it. Under two tags the code runs beneath the
      outer one, and the value beneath both is reached by nesting
      `within`, `within ~(within ~(reverse))`. The code is a quote or a
      path, `within /a`: a value computed at the call,
      `within (reverse)`, is refused under the input. A value under no
      tag runs its code by `apply`, and is refused here.
      ::link~(::any/apply | doc) ::link~(::any/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :code
        |~~ The edit, a quote run against the payload. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :code/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The edit `within` runs is a quote, `within ~(reverse)`; a
            value computed at the call is refused, the error a step
            answered there among them.
        ~~|
    | throws :code/NotGivenError
        |~~ `within` runs the code its call gives beneath the tag,
            `::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse)`; a call with no code is
            refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The value edited beneath its tag, under the tag again. ~~|
        ::tagged
    | throws :SubjectNotTaggedError
        |~~ `within` edits a value beneath its tag; a value under no tag,
            a vector or a plain map, is edited by `apply`,
            `[1 2] | apply ~(reverse)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheTag
        |~~ The edit returns under the tag. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse) | eq ::Box[2 1])
    | proves :editsASetAsItsVector
        |~~ Code runs on a set as on its vector, and its answer comes back
            a set.
        ~~|
        ~(#[3 1] | within ~(get [/0 /1 /0]) | eq #[1 3])
    | proves :laysItsTagAgainOverTheAnswer
        |~~ An answer the tag's invariant refuses is refused as it goes
            back under the tag.
        ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ A number above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0)) | ::Pos#3 | within ~(sub 5) !| tag | eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)
    | proves :keepsItsNamesInside
        |~~ A name the code binds stays inside it. ~~|
        ~(:n 7 | ::Box[1] | within ~(:n 5 | get [~(n)]) | n | eq 7)
    | proves :reachesUnderTwoTags
        |~~ An edit under two tags reaches the innermost payload. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box#[3 1] | within ~(within ~(get [/1 /0 2])) | eq ::Box#[1 2 3])
    | proves :code/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
        ~(#[3 1] | within 1 !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::tagged/within/code/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :SubjectNotTaggedError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector under no tag is refused. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | within ~(reverse)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::tagged/within/SubjectNotTaggedError ::vec])
    | proves :code/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoCode
        |~~ A call with no code is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1] | within !| tag | eq ::tagged/within/code/NotGivenError)
    | proves :checksTheAnswerAlone
        |~~ The steps between may break the tag's invariant, and the
            answer alone is checked.
        ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ A number above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0)) | ::Pos#3 | within ~(sub 5 | add 10) | eq ::Pos#8)
    | impl ::builtin{}

